%I A401507 #38 Nov 23 2026 09:46:35
%S A401507 2,6,12,24,30,48,60,72,96,120,180,192,210,240,288,360,384,420
%N A401507 Numbers k in A025487 such that the equation of x + y = z, can be satisfied by x, y, and z all having the same prime signature as k.
%C A401507 Prime signatures which have 1st powers in it must have some equation x + y = z which satisfy all of x, y, and z have the given prime signature.
%C A401507 Any prime signature which are 3rd or higher perfect power cannot have such equations because of Fermat's Last Theorem. So, 1, 5, 7, 10, 14, 18, 23, and 25 are not in this sequence.
%C A401507 Any prime signature which only contains 2 in it (like {2}, {2, 2}) must not have such equations because in a Pythagorean triple, at least one number has to be divisible by 4, so the three numbers in a Pythagorean triple cannot be all squarefree. So, 3 and 11 are not in this sequence.
%C A401507 19 is not in this sequence as well. A025487(19) = 144 = 2^4 * 3^2, which corresponds to the prime signature of {4,2}, which is just about the Pythagorean triples where all three numbers have the prime signature of {2,1}.
%C A401507 To prove that x^2 + y^2 = z^2, where x, y, and z all with the prime signature of p1^2*p2 does not exist, we should do:
%C A401507 First situation: x, (x^2-1)/2, (x^2+1)/2 (x is in p1^2*p2, also odd)
%C A401507 Second situation: p*4, p^2*4-1, p^2*4+1 (p is prime > 2)
%C A401507 Third situation: p*4, p^2-4, p^2+4 (p is prime > 2)
%C A401507 For first situation, except for x = 63, (x^2-1)/2 must has at 3 omega() function value (that means, omega(n) >= 3), which cannot satisfy the conditions, because we need it to be the form p1^2 * p2. For x=63, (63^2-1)/2 = 1984, which is not the form of p1^2 * p2.
%C A401507 For the second situation, for any p > 5, it is either omega(p^2*4-1)>=3 or the power of 3 much bigger than two, which does not satisfy the conditions.
%C A401507 For the third situation. For p = 3, p^2*4-1=35, p=5, p^2*4+1=101, neither of which satisfy the conditions.
%C A401507 For the third situation, for any p>11, p^2-4 has either omega() >= 3 or has the power of 3 bigger than 2, both of which does not satisfy the condition. For p = 3, 3^2+4=13, p = 5, 5^2+4=29, 7^2+4=53, 11^2+4=125, neither of which satisfies p1^2*p2.
%C A401507 QED
%C A401507 To prove that x^2 + y^2 = z^2, where x, y, and z all have the prime signature of p1^2*p2, does not exist, we have to break down it into three situations.
%C A401507 First situation: x, (x^2-1)/2, (x^2+1)/2 (x has the prime signature of p1^2*p2, and also odd)
%C A401507 Second situation: p*4, p^2*4-1, p^2*4+1 (p is a prime number > 2)
%C A401507 Third situation: p*4, p^2-4, p^2+4 (p is a prime number > 2)
%C A401507 For the first situation, except for x = 63, omega((x^2 - 1)/2) >= 3, which cannot satisfy the conditions, because we need it to be the form p1^2 * p2, which requires having omega = 2. For x = 63, (63^2 - 1)/2 = 1984, which is not the form of p1^2 * p2.
%C A401507 For the second situation, for any p > 5, it is either omega(p^2*4 - 1)>=3 or the power of 3 of (p^2*4 - 1) bigger than two, which does not satisfy the conditions.
%C A401507 For the second situation. For p = 3, p^2*4 - 1 = 35, for p = 5, p^2*4 + 1 = 101, neither of which satisfies the conditions.
%C A401507 For the third situation, for any p > 11, p^2 - 4 has either omega >= 3 or has the power of 3 bigger than 2, both of which does not satisfy the conditions. For p = 3, 3^2 + 4 = 13, for p = 5, 5^2 + 4 = 29, for p = 7, 7^2 + 4 = 53, for p = 11, 11^2 + 4 = 125, neither of which has the prime signature of p1^2*p2.
%e A401507 26 is in this sequence because A025487(26) = 288, and the equation x + y = z, where x, y, and z all have the same prime signature as 288 does exist: 15753125 + 332667 = 16085792, where 15753125 = 5^5 * 71^2, 332667 = 3^5 * 37^2, and 16085792 = 2^5 * 709^2, where 288 = 2^5 * 3^2.
%e A401507 11 is not in this sequence because A025487(11) = 36, and the prime signature of 36 can be also represented as (p1 * p2)^2. It should be a Pythagorean triple, but at least one number in a Pythagorean triple has to be divisible by 4. So, the three numbers in a Pythagorean triple cannot be all squarefree. So, x + y = z, where x, y, and z all have the same prime signature of 36.
%Y A401507 Cf. A025487.
